<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.21240v1/tree_search_comp_plot.svg" id="A2.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/136;" width="274" height="136"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>
Training reward comparison on multi-hop QA between tree search at token/sentence-level and agent step-level.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ B.3 Tree Search at Different Levels ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details ‣ Tree Search for LLM Agent Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ B.3 Tree Search at Different Levels ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details ‣ Tree Search for LLM Agent Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> show that tree search at the token/sentence level performs worse than the agent-step level in both single-hop QA and multi-hop QA, and even falls below the performance of chain-based GRPO.
We attribute this effect to two factors.
On the one hand, exploring branches in the middle of an agent step lacks a clear purpose and may lead to rollout budget waste.
On the other hand, the credit derived from token/sentence-level tree structure undermines the integrity of the agent step, and the resulting process signal instead hinders the learning performance.
<span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Therefore, we argue that tree search conducted at the token or sentence level is not suitable for agent RL tasks.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Performance with Different Tree Structures</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We try different tree-search structures in Table <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.4 Performance with Different Tree Structures ‣ Appendix B Experiment Details ‣ Tree Search for LLM Agent Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
When <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N=0,L=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N=0,L=0)</annotation></semantics></math>, the Tree-GRPO degenerates into vanilla GRPO.
The results show that the impact of <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is not much different.
However, since the expansion iterations <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> directly affect the rollout efficiency (as the iterations are executed serially), we consider using a larger <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and set <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=1</annotation></semantics></math> in most of the experiments.
In addition, while reducing the number of trees <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> saves rollout budget, the more constrained exploration in tree search adversely impacts the performance of RL.
Results indicate that, for different rollout budgets, balancing <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\times L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\times L</annotation></semantics></math> can strike a trade-off between exploration and exploitation in tree search, leading to better performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>
Performance on multi-hop QA with different tree structures.
The base model is Qwen2.5-3b.
The best results are indicated in <span id="A2.T7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.
</figcaption>
<table id="A2.T7.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A2.T7.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T7.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T7.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">M, N, L</span></td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T7.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hotpot</span></td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T7.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2wiki</span></td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T7.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Musiq</span></td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T7.4.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bamb</span></td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A2.T7.4.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg.</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T7.4.2" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#DADCEF;">
<td id="A2.T7.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="6"><span id="A2.T7.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Rollout Token/Tool Budget <math id="A2.T7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#DADCEF;" mathbackground="#DADCEF">≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> 2/per prompt</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.T7.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A2.T7.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A2.T7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(M=2,N=0,L=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(M=2,N=0,L=0)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">39.0</td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">36.3</td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">15.2</td>
<td id="A2.T7.4.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">36.8</td>
